---
scope: src/service/install/installAgents
status: aligned
description: "Installs the canonical AIDE pipeline agents and the generic study-playbook skill into the host project, byte-faithful and skip-on-exists."
intent: >
  Plant the canonical AIDE agent set and the generic study-playbook
  skill into a host project so each pipeline phase has a runtime
  identity to delegate to. The category iterates the canonical
  registry, lands one file per registered agent plus the skill,
  preserves any host customization untouched, and reports per-file
  status. It writes byte-faithful copies of canonical content; it
  invents none. Configuration variance — including how the brain is
  reached — lives entirely in the host's own configuration; this
  category never branches on it.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Every canonical agent enumerated by the install service's content registry lands in the host project, with the generic study-playbook skill installed alongside. Adding a new canonical agent is a single registry entry — this category picks it up automatically.
    - Agent and skill content flow through the install service's canonical-content chokepoint byte-faithfully. This category stages files; it never authors agent prose or skill prose.
    - Each landed file reports status — created, exists, or skipped — under the install service's reporting pattern. A re-run on a fully-installed project changes nothing and reports every file as existing.
    - Host customizations to any landed agent file or to the study-playbook skill survive every subsequent run. Upgrading installed canonical content is a separate action's job, not this category's.
  undesired:
    - This category overwriting, patching, or migrating an agent file or the study-playbook skill that already exists in the host project. Customizations are the host's surface; this category respects them on every run after the first.
    - Agent or skill content authored in this category as inline strings instead of read through the install service's canonical-content chokepoint. That creates a second source of truth that drifts from canonical.
    - This category branching on the host's brain configuration — inspecting the brain config, varying which agents land, or mutating an agent's declared MCP server list per host. Every host receives the same canonical files; configuration variance lives in the host's brain config, not here.
    - This category restating brain-MCP-key portability doctrine, model-assignment rationale, or pipeline-phase-to-agent mapping in its own outcomes or strategy. That doctrine is owned upstream — the canonical agent files carry it, not this category.
---
